\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Identification pattern across the velocity sweep. Mass is identified accurately even at low speed, because the translational acceleration excites the $f\bm  {a}$ column of the regressor $\Phi $. The actuator time constants $\hat  \tau _{rc}$ and $\hat  \tau _f$ converge monotonically toward their true values as $v_{peak}$ increases: at low excitation the rate-loop and thrust-loop are barely commanded so their RLS residuals carry little information; above $v_{peak}\!\approx \!10$\,m/s both estimates saturate close to the offline-identified $\tau _{rc}\!\approx \!56$\,ms and $\tau _f\!\approx \!9$\,ms.}}{6}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:vsweep}{{1}{6}{Identification pattern across the velocity sweep. Mass is identified accurately even at low speed, because the translational acceleration excites the $f\vect a$ column of the regressor $\Phi $. The actuator time constants $\hat \tau _{rc}$ and $\hat \tau _f$ converge monotonically toward their true values as $v_{peak}$ increases: at low excitation the rate-loop and thrust-loop are barely commanded so their RLS residuals carry little information; above $v_{peak}\!\approx \!10$\,m/s both estimates saturate close to the offline-identified $\tau _{rc}\!\approx \!56$\,ms and $\tau _f\!\approx \!9$\,ms}{figure.1}{}}
